\subsection{Bound the Number of Local Picks before Checking Arrivals}

Work stealing balances uneven queues, but a busy worker can
neglect globally submitted tasks even as it completes local
work. Consider a single worker with a private deque, a shared
FIFO for external arrivals, and a local-pick quota $Q>0$.
The accounting unit here is a dispatched continuation slice,
not an entire guest task. Queue removals are synchronized,
each slice has one owner, and requeued tasks enter exactly one
queue. Assume slices eventually return to dispatch. This rule
provides a bound on how long the worker ignores the global
queue, not a bound on the work already queued there.

\begin{sudocode}[Choose one continuation at a dispatch boundary]
\If{$streak\ge Q$ or local deque is empty}
  \State $streak\gets 0$ \Comment{Start a new polling window}
  \State $g\gets\Call{PopGlobalFIFO}{}$
  \If{$g$ exists}
    \State \Return $g$ \Comment{Oldest external arrival first}
  \EndIf
\EndIf
\State $t\gets\Call{PopLocal}{}$
\If{$t$ exists}
  \State $streak\gets streak+1$
  \State \Return $t$
\EndIf
\State \Return idle
\end{sudocode}

Set $Q=2$ and arrange for external task $G$ to arrive just
after an empty global poll, while an unending supply of local
slices is available. The next dispatches are $L_1,L_2,G$.
Without the quota, the worker might pick a third local slice
instead. Set each local slice to a synthetic $5\,\mathrm{ms}$
and ignore dispatch overhead: $G$ starts after
$10\,\mathrm{ms}$. If a task $F$ precedes it in the FIFO, then
the sequence can be $L_1,L_2,F,L_3,L_4,G$; the quota does
not skip existing arrivals.

\Cref{fig:visual-247} places the arrival just after the empty poll. The quota limits the number of local slices that can follow it before another poll is required.

