Between global polls there are at most $Q$ local dispatches.
That invariant is independent of guest time spent per slice:
one blocking native call can still violate any wall-time bound.
An idle worker additionally needs a wakeup protocol on external
enqueue. In a multiworker implementation, extend the accounting
to stolen slices and test visibility and idle wakeups separately
from this local preference rule.

\section{Preemption, priority, and latency}

Cooperative execution needs polls in code that can otherwise run indefinitely. Polling at backward branches bounds work in simple loops only if individual instructions and calls are themselves bounded. A GC request likewise needs a thread to reach a state in which its roots are visible, or a protocol for safely treating it as outside managed code. A priority runtime also faces inversion: Yi Qiu, Puxiang Xiong, and Tianlong Zhu's \emph{The Design and Implementation of the RT-Thread Operating System} illustrates priority inheritance for mutexes. Raising the lock owner's priority can limit preemption by intermediate-priority tasks while a high-priority task waits. It does not bound a critical section that can block indefinitely. Measure time to poll, ready-queue delay, and lock wait separately before attributing a missed deadline to the scheduler.

Chapter~23 of the Modern Compiler Dossier, \emph{Compiler as an Actor System}, applies message-driven scheduling to compilation tasks. The same distinction holds there: ordering guarantees at a mailbox do not determine when a worker runs its next message.
